  "name": "ADRENALINE (EPINEPHRINE) PLASMA",
  "code": "1015",
  "cost": 0,
  "interpretation": "<p data-start=\"168\" data-end=\"512\">The <strong data-start=\"172\" data-end=\"207\">plasma adrenaline (epinephrine)</strong> test measures the concentration of epinephrine in the bloodstream. Epinephrine is a hormone and neurotransmitter produced by the <strong data-start=\"337\" data-end=\"356\">adrenal medulla</strong> in response to stress, and it plays a key role in the <strong data-start=\"411\" data-end=\"441\">sympathetic nervous system</strong>, affecting heart rate, blood pressure, blood glucose levels, and more.</p>\n<p data-start=\"514\" data-end=\"850\">This test is primarily used to help diagnose <strong data-start=\"559\" data-end=\"579\">pheochromocytoma</strong> and <strong data-start=\"584\" data-end=\"601\">paraganglioma</strong>, which are rare tumors that secrete excess catecholamines (like adrenaline and norepinephrine). It may also be used in evaluating unexplained <strong data-start=\"744\" data-end=\"760\">hypertension</strong>, <strong data-start=\"762\" data-end=\"789\">sudden anxiety episodes</strong>, <strong data-start=\"791\" data-end=\"806\">tachycardia</strong>, <strong data-start=\"808\" data-end=\"820\">sweating</strong>, and <strong data-start=\"826\" data-end=\"849\">panic-like symptoms</strong>.</p>\n<p data-start=\"904\" data-end=\"952\">High levels of plasma adrenaline may indicate:</p>\n<ul data-start=\"953\" data-end=\"1601\">\n<li data-start=\"953\" data-end=\"1053\">\n<p data-start=\"955\" data-end=\"1053\"><strong data-start=\"955\" data-end=\"975\">Pheochromocytoma</strong> or <strong data-start=\"979\" data-end=\"996\">paraganglioma</strong> &ndash; especially if elevation is significant and sustained</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"1054\" data-end=\"1146\">\n<p data-start=\"1056\" data-end=\"1146\"><strong data-start=\"1056\" data-end=\"1084\">Severe stress or anxiety</strong> &ndash; emotional or physical stress can transiently raise levels</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"1147\" data-end=\"1230\">\n<p data-start=\"1149\" data-end=\"1230\"><strong data-start=\"1149\" data-end=\"1165\">Hypoglycemia</strong> &ndash; triggers epinephrine release as a counter-regulatory hormone</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"1231\" data-end=\"1302\">\n<p data-start=\"1233\" data-end=\"1302\"><strong data-start=\"1233\" data-end=\"1260\">Acute illness or trauma</strong> &ndash; especially in critically ill patients</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"1303\" data-end=\"1347\">\n<p data-start=\"1305\" data-end=\"1347\"><strong data-start=\"1305\" data-end=\"1345\">Withdrawal from sedatives or alcohol</strong></p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"1348\" data-end=\"1447\">\n<p data-start=\"1350\" data-end=\"1447\"><strong data-start=\"1350\" data-end=\"1371\">Use of stimulants</strong> &ndash; such as caffeine, nicotine, amphetamines, or certain asthma medications</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"1448\" data-end=\"1521\">\n<p data-start=\"1450\" data-end=\"1521\"><strong data-start=\"1450\" data-end=\"1470\">Postural changes</strong> &ndash; standing before blood draw can increase levels</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"1522\" data-end=\"1601\">\n<p data-start=\"1524\" data-end=\"1601\"><strong data-start=\"1524\" data-end=\"1539\">Medications</strong> &ndash; like MAO inhibitors, tricyclic antidepressants, or levodopa</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p data-start=\"1603\" data-end=\"1827\">A significantly elevated value, especially in a calm, resting, and fasting state, raises strong suspicion of <strong data-start=\"1712\" data-end=\"1746\">catecholamine-secreting tumors</strong> and usually warrants follow-up with <strong data-start=\"1783\" data-end=\"1807\">plasma metanephrines</strong> or imaging studies.</p>",
